  "style": {
    "id": "constructivist-poster",
    "name": "Constructivist Poster",
    "description": "A 1920s Constructivist poster that builds itself with force: a red wedge drives diagonally into a white circle, heavy black bars slam in on a 35 degree axis, a black cone throws words that grow as they fly, wheels roll along a bar and stop dead against the circle, and blocks of condensed capitals swing in on hinges. Cream lithograph paper, red / black / one grey, mottled ink and slight misregistration. Every element is a thick painted card at its own height on hidden dowels, so a hard key light throws offset shadows; once the poster locks, an orthographic camera tilts to reveal the layered relief, then snaps back flat. Motion is percussive (hard ease-in, dead stop, tiny ring) and every move has a mechanical hit from the same build plan.",
